  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nUpdate...\nAdditional showers and thunderstorms - scattered in coverage are\nexpected to develop this aftn associated with another impulse\nmoving into Indiana. With scattered coverage, some local heavy rain\nremains possible but the widespread heavy rain threat has \ndiminished, Therefore, the flood watch has been allowed to expire. \n\n\nPrevious...\nThunderstorms developing in association with another \nimpulse across Illinois and Missouri will move across the area this \nafternoon. This will also be in conjunctions with a pre-frontal \ntrough. Precipitable water will still be high, but the warm cloud \nlayer will not be quite as deep. So there may still be locally heavy \nrain, but the flooding threat appears to be more isolated. In \naddition, with moderate instability there could be some more robust \nstorms that could produce strong to damaging winds.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nLow amplitude short wave will push a cold front into the area\ntonight. This will become elongated in a north northeast to south\nsouthwest orientation which will cause the boundary to be slow to\nmove all of the way through the region. There will still be some\npotential for showers and storms until this front clears. \n\nBroad long wave trough will move into the Great Lakes towards mid\nweek. This will push another cold front through late Wednesday into\nWednesday night. There will be a bigger change in air mass with highs\nwill be in the 70s with lows in the 50s to lower 60s for the latter \npart of the week.",
